WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:08.641
GPUI is the GPU rendered Rust UI framework behind the Zed editor. It expects you to write your interface in Rust.

00:09.391 --> 00:14.523
This casting player runs in a GPUI window, and contains no Rust of its own.

00:14.723 --> 00:19.784
Its whole interface is one data file: inputs, state machines, a view, and skins.

00:20.534 --> 00:30.517
egui redraws Rust code every frame. Slint has its own markup. Tauri puts a web front end in a web view. GPUI uses Rust views.

00:30.717 --> 00:39.895
A UI machine writes every state down and names every transition, so the interface can be checked, tested without a window, and reskinned without code.

00:40.645 --> 00:45.566
The player has four layers that run in parallel. Each has exactly one current state.

00:45.766 --> 00:50.054
Entering connecting requests cast.start, with the file and the receiver.

00:50.254 --> 00:55.707
When the TV reports playing, the layer moves itself. The document holds no code at all.

00:56.457 --> 01:02.601
The Osprey code that remains answers each request, and feeds what the TV reports back in as inputs.

01:02.801 --> 01:04.972
It never decides what the screen shows.

01:05.722 --> 01:13.023
The whole document is checked when it loads. A transition to a misspelled state is refused, with its exact JSON path.

01:13.223 --> 01:15.902
So is an initial state that does not exist.

01:16.652 --> 01:24.678
Nothing is partly applied. A cycle is reported instead of spinning, and a refused document shows its problems in place of the app.

01:25.428 --> 01:33.987
What to know first. The player runs in the GPUI host on macOS today, and the two skins without a canvas still need layout work.

01:34.737 --> 01:42.027
Build Osprey, and open the player. Osprey is actively evolving. Try it on your projects, and read the UI machines spec.
